[TITLE:e200z4 Manual Extracted Data - Critical Specifications]

[INTRO]
This document contains key specifications extracted directly from the e200z4 Core
Reference Manual (e200z4RM, Rev. 0, 10/2009) for validation against LLVM implementation.

MANUAL_SOURCE: https://www.elektronikjk.com/elementy_czynne/IC/E200Z4.pdf
EXTRACTION_DATE: 2025-11-02
EXTRACTION_METHOD: pdftotext + manual review

[CHAPTER-4-DUAL-ISSUE-PAIRING]
UID: CH4-PAIRING

TITLE: Dual-Issue Pairing Rules from Table 4-1

LOCATION: Chapter 4, Table 4-1 (Page 4-2)

EXTRACTED_TABLE: Concurrent Instruction Issue Capabilities

Instruction pairs marked with "4" can dual-issue together.
Instruction pairs marked with "—" cannot dual-issue together.

Row (First Instruction)  | Branch | Load/Store | Scalar Int | Scalar Float | Vector Int | Vector Float | Special
Column (Second Instruction)|        |            |            |              |            |               |
---------------------------|--------|------------|------------|--------------|------------|---------------|----------
Branch                     |   —    |     4      |     4      |      4       |     4      |       4       |    —
Load/Store                 |   4    |     —      |     4      |      4       |     4      |       4       |    —
Scalar Integer             |   4    |     4      |     41     |      4       |     42     |       4       |    —
Scalar Float               |   4    |     4      |     4      |      4       |     4      |       —       |    —
Vector Integer             |   4    |     4      |     42     |      4       |     43     |       4       |    —
Vector Float               |   4    |     4      |     4      |      —       |     4      |       —       |    —
Special                    |   —    |     —      |     —      |      —       |     —      |       —       |    —

FOOTNOTES:
1: Excludes divide class instructions occurring in both issue slots
2: Excludes vector MAC/multiply class instructions occurring with scalar multiply, 
   or divide class instructions occurring in both issue slots
3: Excludes vector MAC/multiply class instructions occurring in both issue slots, 
   or divide class instructions occurring in both issue slots

KEY_RESTRICTIONS:
- Two divide instructions cannot issue simultaneously (single divide unit, blocking)
- Vector MAC/multiply cannot pair with scalar multiply
- Special instructions cannot pair with anything

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ NOT IMPLEMENTED
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE: CompleteModel = 0, no explicit pairing rule enforcement

[CHAPTER-4-TIMING]
UID: CH4-TIMING

TITLE: Instruction Timing Specifications

LOCATION: Chapter 4.3 (Execution Units)

INTEGER_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Simple integer (adds, subtracts, compares, shifts, rotates, count leading zeros): 
  1 cycle latency, single cycle execution
  
- Multiply instructions: 
  Latency: 2 cycles
  Throughput: 1 per cycle (pipelined)
  
- Divide instructions:
  Latency: Variable 4-14 cycles (worst case: 14 cycles)
  Throughput: Blocking (pipeline unavailable during divide)

LOAD_STORE_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Load instructions:
  Latency: 2 cycles
  Throughput: 1 per cycle (maximum)
  Load-to-use: Single stall cycle if dependent
  
- Store instructions:
  Latency: 2 cycles  
  Throughput: 1 per cycle (maximum)
  Store data can be fed-forward from immediately preceding load (no stall)

FLOATING_POINT_INSTRUCTIONS (SPE/EFPU):
- FP add, subtract, compare, multiply, multiply-accumulate:
  Latency: 2 cycles
  Throughput: 1 per cycle
  
- SPE floating-point divide:
  Latency: 13 cycles
  Throughput: Blocking (pipeline unavailable during divide)

BRANCH_INSTRUCTIONS:
- Conditional branches (not taken): 1 clock cycle
- Branches with successful BTB target prefetching: 1 clock cycle (if correctly predicted)
- All other taken branches: 2 clock cycles
- Branch misprediction penalty: 3 cycles (mentioned in code)

VALIDATION_STATUS:
- ✓ Integer simple: 1 cycle - MATCHES (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:66)
- ✓ Multiply: 2 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 4 cycles, manual says 2)
- ✓ Divide: 4-14 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 34 cycles, manual says worst case 14)
- ✓ Load: 2 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 3 cycles)
- ✓ Store: 2 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 3 cycles)
- ✓ FP general: 2 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 3 cycles)
- ✓ FP divide: 13 cycles - DISCREPANCY (code says 25 cycles)

[CHAPTER-4-PIPELINE]
UID: CH4-PIPELINE

TITLE: Pipeline Stages

LOCATION: Chapter 4.4, Table 4-2

STAGES:
1. IFETCH - Instruction Fetch From Memory
2. DECODE/RF READ/FF/MEM EA - Instruction Decode/Register Read/Operand Forwarding/Memory Effective Address Generation
3. EXECUTE0/MEM0 - Instruction Execution stage 0/Memory Access stage 0
4. EXECUTE1/MEM1 - Instruction Execution stage 1/Memory Access stage 1
5. WB - Write Back to Registers

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ PARTIAL MATCH
- Code has: IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB (5 stages)
- Manual has: IFETCH, DECODE/RF READ/FF/MEM EA, EXECUTE0/MEM0, EXECUTE1/MEM1, WB
- Note: Manual shows dual-execute stages (EXECUTE0/MEM0, EXECUTE1/MEM1) which may be for dual-issue

[CHAPTER-5-IVOR-TABLE]
UID: CH5-IVOR

TITLE: IVOR Register Table

LOCATION: Chapter 1, Table 1-2 and Chapter 5.6

EXTRACTED_IVOR_TABLE:
IVORn    | Interrupt Type                          | Notes
---------|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------
None     | System reset (not an interrupt)         | Vector to [p_rstbase[0:29]] || 0xFFC
0        | Critical input                          | Autovectored (uses IVOR0)
1        | Machine check                           | 
         | Machine check (non-maskable interrupt)  |
2        | Data storage                            |
3        | Instruction storage                     |
4        | External input                          | Autovectored (uses IVOR4)
5        | Alignment                               |
6        | Program                                 |
7        | Floating-point unavailable               |
8        | System call                             |
9        | AP unavailable                          | Not used by e200z4 core
10       | Decrementer                             |
11       | Fixed-interval timer                     |
12       | Watchdog timer                          |
13       | Data TLB error                          |
14       | Instruction TLB error                   |
15       | Debug                                   |
16-31    | Reserved                                |
32       | SPE unavailable                         |
33       | SPE data exception                       |
34       | SPE round exception                      |

NOTES:
- Critical input (IVOR0) and External input (IVOR4) can be autovectored
- Vectored interrupts supply interrupt vector offset directly

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ NEEDS IMPLEMENTATION
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE: No differentiation between interrupt types in context save logic

[CHAPTER-5-REGISTER-SAVE]
UID: CH5-REGSAVE

TITLE: Register Save Requirements for Interrupts

LOCATION: Chapter 5.7 (Interrupt Definitions)

NOTE: Manual sections 5.7.1-5.7.20 describe each interrupt type but register save
requirements may be in exception entry sequence descriptions.

HARDWARE_AUTOMATIC_SAVES:
On interrupt entry, hardware automatically saves:
- SRR0 (Save/Restore Register 0): Instruction address
- SRR1 (Save/Restore Register 1): Machine State Register (MSR) value

SOFTWARE_SAVE_REQUIREMENTS:
Based on Power ISA embedded category and e200z4 architecture:

REQUIRED_FOR_ALL_INTERRUPTS:
- LR (Link Register) - Return address
- CR (Condition Register) - Processor state
- XER (Fixed-Point Exception Register) - If modified/used
- CTR (Count Register) - If used
- GPRs (General-Purpose Registers) - All used registers

REQUIRED_FOR_FPU_SPE_INTERRUPTS (if FPU/SPE used):
- FPRs (Floating-Point Registers) - All used FPRs
- SPEFSCR (SPE Floating-Point Status and Control Register) - Required if FPU operations present

NOTE: Need to extract detailed table from manual sections 5.7.1 and 5.7.5 for
critical vs external interrupt specific requirements.

VALIDATION_STATUS: ⚠ PARTIAL IMPLEMENTATION
LLVM_CURRENT_STATE:
- ✓ LR save: Implemented
- ✓ CR save: Implemented  
- ✓ GPR save: Implemented (based on usage)
- ✓ XER save: Implemented (detection added)
- ✓ CTR save: Implemented (detection added)
- ✓ FPR save: Detection added, code generation missing
- ✓ SPEFSCR save: Detection added, code generation missing
- ✗ SPR save: Only XER/CTR handled, others may be needed
- ✗ Interrupt type differentiation: Not implemented

[CRITICAL-DISCREPANCIES]
UID: DISCREPANCIES

DISCREPANCY_001: Multiply Instruction Latency
Manual: 2 cycles
Code: 4 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:101-108)
Impact: WCET analysis will be pessimistic (2x slower)
Priority: HIGH

DISCREPANCY_002: Divide Instruction Latency  
Manual: 4-14 cycles (worst case 14 cycles)
Code: 34 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:94-100)
Impact: WCET analysis will be highly pessimistic (2.4x slower)
Priority: CRITICAL

DISCREPANCY_003: Load Instruction Latency
Manual: 2 cycles
Code: 3 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:169-175)
Impact: WCET analysis will be pessimistic
Priority: HIGH

DISCREPANCY_004: Store Instruction Latency
Manual: 2 cycles
Code: 3 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:192-197)
Impact: WCET analysis will be pessimistic
Priority: HIGH

DISCREPANCY_005: FP General Instruction Latency
Manual: 2 cycles
Code: 3 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:240-247)
Impact: WCET analysis will be pessimistic
Priority: MEDIUM

DISCREPANCY_006: FP Divide Instruction Latency
Manual: 13 cycles
Code: 25 cycles (PPCScheduleE200Z4.td:262-268)
Impact: WCET analysis will be highly pessimistic (1.9x slower)
Priority: HIGH

DISCREPANCY_007: Dual-Issue Pairing Rules Not Modeled
Manual: Table 4-1 defines explicit pairing rules
Code: CompleteModel = 0, no pairing rule enforcement
Impact: Scheduler may create invalid instruction pairs
Priority: CRITICAL (blocks CERT-001)

